+<div class="research-note-list"><article class="research-note"><h3>Interview 1 Student with a Tight Class Schedule</h3><ul><li>Behavior: Had only 35 minutes between classes and chose the food court because it was closer.</li><li>Behavior: Encountered a long line and bought a packaged sandwich instead of the preferred meal.</li><li>Opinion: Prioritized time over food choice and was willing to compromise on the meal.</li><li>Behavior: Usually does not research familiar dining locations. Uses the university dining website to check hours and maps to estimate walking distance.</li><li>Behavior: Sometimes visits a location without confirming service because checking takes additional time.</li><li>Behavior: Visited a cafe that was open after its kitchen had stopped serving. The unsuccessful trip took about 12 minutes, and the student went without eating.</li><li>Opinion: Considers university information more trustworthy than map listings when operating hours conflict.</li><li>Opinion: Would consider a new tool if it helped avoid unsuccessful trips and find food within a short break.</li><li>Key finding: Limited time can lead students to accept less desirable meals or visit without confirming service. Location hours alone may not provide enough information for a successful dining decision.</li></ul></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Interview 2 Student Balancing Budget and Dietary Preferences</h3><ul><li>Behavior: Selected a vegetarian bowl shown online but found it sold out upon arrival.</li><li>Behavior: Went off campus for an alternative, walked farther, spent about $4 more, and used most of the break.</li><li>Opinion: Prioritized a suitable, filling meal over price and convenience.</li><li>Behavior: Checks menus and compares prices before choosing where to eat.</li><li>Behavior: Asks employees for clarification when ingredient information is unclear.</li><li>Behavior: Chose a different meal when an online menu did not list the ingredients in a sauce.</li><li>Behavior: Asked an employee to resolve conflicting descriptions of a menu item.</li><li>Opinion: Would consider a new tool that provided accurate ingredient information and current food availability before walking to a location.</li><li>Key finding: Scheduled menus and incomplete ingredient descriptions can leave students unable to confirm a suitable meal. The consequences may include additional spending, walking, and lost break time.</li></ul></article><article class="research-note"><h3>Interview 3 Student Who Prefers Familiar Routines</h3><ul><li>Behavior: Usually visits a familiar cafe after class because it is already along the student’s route.</li><li>Behavior: Does not check online before regular visits and chooses from the food available upon arrival.</li><li>Opinion: Prioritizes convenience and minimal effort over finding a particular item.</li><li>Behavior: Uses maps to check directions and hours when visiting an unfamiliar location.</li><li>Behavior: Was unsure about school-break hours but walked past the cafe anyway because it was on the way.</li><li>Behavior: Found no pastries available on one visit, bought coffee, and ate food from their bag.</li><li>Opinion: Considered the unavailable pastries a minor inconvenience.</li><li>Opinion: Trusts date-specific campus announcements more than regular map hours for holiday or school-break schedules.</li><li>Opinion: Might use a new tool to check a specific item or unusual hours, but probably would not check it before every visit.</li></ul></article></div></div></details><details class="interview-question"><summary>Salvador Rodarte — Interview notes</summary><div class="interview-answer"><p>These notes haven’t been added yet.</p></div></details><details class="interview-question"><summary>Daniel Lozano — Interview notes</summary><div class="interview-answer"><p>These notes haven’t been added yet.</p></div></details><details class="interview-question"><summary>Jacob Ordonez — Interview notes</summary><div class="i