 <section id="competitor-analysis" class="charter-section" aria-labelledby="competitor-analysis-title"><header class="charter-section__heading"><h2 id="competitor-analysis-title">Competitor analysis</h2></header><div class="prose">
-<div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button--outline" href="{{ '/assets/pdfs/sprint-3-competitors.pdf' | relative_url }}">Read original PDF</a><a class="button button--orange" href="{{ '/assets/pdfs/sprint-3-competitors.pdf' | relative_url }}" download>Download Competitor analysis · PDF</a></div>
+<div class="hero-actions"><a class="button button--outline" href="{{ '/assets/pdfs/sprint-3-competitors.pdf' | relative_url }}">Open PDF</a><a class="button button--orange" href="{{ '/assets/pdfs/sprint-3-competitors.pdf' | relative_url }}" download>Download Competitor analysis · PDF</a></div>
 <div class="competitor-card-list"><article class="competitor-card"><h3>Competitor 1: UTEP Dining Website</h3>
 <p><strong>Type:</strong> Direct competitor and existing campus alternative.</p>
 <p><strong>What it offers:</strong> The portal lists dining locations and hours and includes a “What’s Open Now” section. The Pick N’ Shovel page also provides a menu date selector, nutrition information, and dietary and allergen filters.</p>
 <p><strong>Walkthrough observation:</strong> I opened the hours page, selected Pick N’ Shovel, changed the menu date, and enabled the vegetarian filter. The filter accepted the selection, but the reviewed menu area did not provide enough item information to confirm current availability.</p>
 <p><strong>Strength:</strong> It already brings campus-specific information together. DineCheck cannot claim that campus hours or dietary filters are unique features.</p>
-<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The reviewed menu area did not clearly distinguish scheduled menu items from food currently available at the counter. There was not enough item information to verify availability before visiting.</p>
+<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The menu area I reviewed didn’t make it clear whether the listed food was still available at the counter.</p>
 <p><strong>Question to investigate:</strong> Can students distinguish a scheduled menu from food that is actually available at the counter?</p>
 <p><strong>Possible DineCheck advantage:</strong> Clearly distinguish location open, counter serving, and item available, with visible update times. This advantage still needs testing.</p>
 </article><article class="competitor-card"><h3>Competitor 2: Nutrislice</h3>
 <p><strong>Type:</strong> Direct competitor offering digital dining menus. Whether it is available at UTEP still needs to be confirmed.</p>
 <p><strong>What it offers:</strong> The University of New Hampshire’s implementation provides daily menus, food descriptions, nutrition information, allergen and dietary filters, a nutrition calculator, and favorite locations.</p>
 <p><strong>Walkthrough limitation:</strong> This review used UNH’s published feature descriptions. It did not verify how a live campus menu displays sold-out items, update times, or outdated information.</p>
-<p><strong>Strength:</strong> Its documented menus, nutrition tools, and filters overlap with DineCheck’s proposed features. DineCheck cannot claim these features are unique.</p>
-<p><strong>Weakness to investigate:</strong> The reviewed documentation does not establish whether students can distinguish scheduled menu items from food currently available at the counter. This is an evidence gap, not a confirmed platform weakness.</p>
+<p><strong>Strength:</strong> Nutrislice already lists many of the features we’re considering, including menus, nutrition tools, and dietary filters.</p>
+<p><strong>Weakness to investigate:</strong> The documentation I read didn’t explain how current item availability appears. I’d need to check a live menu before calling this a weakness.</p>
 <p><strong>Question to investigate:</strong> Does a campus implementation clearly communicate sold-out items, when availability was last updated, and when information may be outdated?</p>
 <p><strong>Possible DineCheck advantage:</strong> A simpler, campus-specific workflow that clearly separates location open, counter serving, and item available, with visible update times. This advantage still needs testing.</p>
-<p><strong>Where DineCheck falls short:</strong> DineCheck is currently a proposed solution and has not demonstrated dependable availability updates or a working staff update process. It also has not shown that its workflow is easier to use than Nutrislice’s existing tools.</p>
+<p><strong>Where DineCheck falls short:</strong> We still need to build and test a reliable update process. We also haven’t shown that DineCheck would be easier to use than Nutrislice.</p>
 </article><article class="competitor-card"><h3>Competitor 3: Google Maps</h3>
 <p><strong>Type:</strong> Indirect competitor; a general restaurant-discovery and navigation alternative.</p>
 <p><strong>What it offers:</strong> Google Maps helps users find restaurants and get directions. Restaurant profiles can include opening status, hours, addresses, reviews, website links, and menu information.</p>
 <p><strong>Walkthrough observation:</strong> I searched for restaurants near UTEP and opened Crave Kitchen &amp; Bar. The profile displayed opening status, hours, an address, reviews, directions, and a restaurant website link. The session also displayed a limited-view notice, which restricted the review.</p>
 <p><strong>Strength:</strong> It combines restaurant discovery with practical information students need to choose and reach a location, including options on and off campus.</p>
-<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The reviewed profile did not establish whether a particular food item was currently available. Opening status and general restaurant information alone were insufficient to confirm item availability.</p>
+<p><strong>Weakness:</strong> The profile I reviewed showed whether the restaurant was open, but I couldn’t confirm whether a specific food item was still available.</p>
 <p><strong>Question to investigate:</strong> Can students confirm that a particular food item is available without leaving Maps or contacting the restaurant?</p>
 <p><strong>Possible DineCheck advantage:</strong> Staff-confirmed campus item availability presented alongside dining hours and visible update times. A campus-specific workflow could help students decide where to eat between classes, but this advantage still needs testing.</p>
-<p><strong>Where DineCheck falls short:</strong> DineCheck’s proposed scope covers campus dining locations. Google Maps supports a broader search for restaurants on and off campus and combines discovery with directions and reviews. DineCheck has also not yet demonstrated a dependable process for keeping item availability current.</p>
+<p><strong>Where DineCheck falls short:</strong> Our plan only covers campus dining. Maps helps students find places on and off campus, get directions, and read reviews. We still need to show that we can keep DineCheck’s item availability accurate.</p>
 </article></div></div></section>
